[Compound Rhyming]
Multiple words rhyme together:
- "out of sight" / "into the night"
- "holding on" / "moving along"

[AVOIDING RHYMING PROBLEMS]

✗ Forced rhyme (choosing wrong word to fit rhyme scheme)
✗ Repetitive rhyming (same rhymes every section)
✗ Obvious rhyming (overused, clichéd pairs)
✗ Pronunciation awkwardness (contorting for rhyme)
✗ Meaning confusion (sacrificing clarity for rhyme)

✓ Natural phrasing with organic rhyme
✓ Varied rhyme schemes across sections
✓ Fresh, creative rhyme combinations
✓ Singable pronunciation
✓ Meaning and clarity prioritized

[RHYME CHECKLIST]

When writing lyrics, verify:
□ Rhyme scheme is intentional
□ Rhymes sound natural when spoken
□ Words still convey intended meaning
□ Rhyme pattern matches song section
□ No forced or awkward phrasing
□ Internal rhymes add musicality
□ Rhyming words are emphasized
□ Genre conventions respected
□ Multiple listens still sound fresh
□ Rhymes advance emotional impact

[PRACTICE EXERCISES]

1. Write 4-line verse with AABB scheme
2. Write 4-line verse with ABAB scheme
3. Add internal rhymes to existing lyrics
4. Replace obvious rhymes with creative slant rhymes
5. Write chorus with repetitive rhyming
6. Study professional song rhyme schemes
7. Sing rhymed lyrics to test naturalness

Your rhyming mastery develops through practice and study!
